    bottomLine:
      "The best flat brush for coating large surfaces with water-based polyurethane. The Nylox filaments practically eliminate brush marks on their own.",
    description:
      "Purdy's Nylox Glide represents the company's dedicated answer to the specific challenges of water-based finish application. Unlike their blended brushes that work with both oil and water formulas, the Nylox line uses 100 percent solid-round nylon filaments that are engineered exclusively for waterborne products. The result is a brush that behaves almost like a fabric applicator — it lays down water-based polyurethane in a thin, uniform sheet without the ridged tracks that plague stiffer bristles. During our testing on a white oak dining table, the Purdy Nylox Glide produced a finish so smooth that even under raking side light, individual brush strokes were essentially invisible after the poly self-leveled. The key technical detail is the rounded edge profile. Where most flat brushes have squared-off edges that leave visible track lines at the brush boundary, the Nylox Glide's bristles are trimmed in a subtle convex curve that feathers each stroke into the next. This is enormously helpful when coating wide panels where overlapping passes are unavoidable. The 3-inch width is ideal for tabletops, shelving, cabinet panels, and stair treads — surfaces where coverage speed matters but precision still counts. The only genuine downside is that pure nylon bristles can soften excessively in very hot or humid conditions, losing some of their spring and loading capacity. In air-conditioned shop environments, this is a non-issue. For outdoor application on a 95-degree day, you might notice the bristles feeling a bit limp. At approximately $14-18 per brush, the Purdy Nylox Glide is a specialist tool that does its specific job better than any generalist brush on the market.",
  },
  {
    id: "wooster-chinex-fps",
    name: "Wooster Chinex FTP 2-Inch Flat",
    category: "Chinex Synthetic Brush",
    badge: "Best for Fine Detail",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 5900,
    pros: [
      "DuPont Chinex bristles maintain stiffness in water without swelling",
      "Excellent shape retention for precision edges and detail work",
      "Ideal for trim, moldings, and intricate furniture details",
      "Handles both water-based and oil-based finishes equally well",
      "Superior bristle memory springs back to shape after every stroke",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Stiffer bristles require more careful technique to avoid marks on flat surfaces",
      "Smaller 2-inch size is slow for large surface area projects",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best precision brush for applying water-based polyurethane on trim, moldings, and furniture details where control matters more than speed.",
    description:
      "The Wooster Chinex FTP occupies a unique and important niche in the water-based polyurethane brush lineup. While softer nylon and polyester brushes excel at laying down smooth, mark-free coats on flat surfaces, they can feel floppy and imprecise when you need to cut a sharp edge along a piece of trim or work finish into the flutes of a turned table leg. The Chinex FTP solves this problem. DuPont Chinex is a synthetic filament specifically engineered to resist the softening and swelling that regular nylon bristles experience when soaked in water-based products. Where a standard nylon brush gradually loses its edge definition over a long finishing session as the bristles absorb water and go limp, Chinex maintains its crisp, firm edge from the first stroke to the last. During our trim finishing test on a set of poplar crown molding pieces, the Chinex FTP cut cleaner lines along edges and deposited finish more precisely into coves and profiles than any other brush we tested. The trade-off is real, though: on flat, open surfaces like tabletops or cabinet doors, the firmer Chinex bristles are more likely to leave visible brush tracks compared to the softer Nylox or Silver Tip. You can mitigate this with proper technique — light pressure, generous loading, and tipping off with just the bristle tips — but it requires more skill. The 2-inch flat width is deliberately compact for detail work. If you are finishing baseboards, window casings, stair balusters, or furniture with ornate details, the Wooster Chinex FTP gives you brush control that softer bristles simply cannot match. At $10-14 per brush, it is a reasonable investment for a specialist tool that fills a genuine gap in your finishing toolkit.",
  },
  {
    id: "corona-chinex-edge",
    name: "Corona Chinex Edge 2.5-Inch Angle",
    category: "Chinex Synthetic Angle Brush",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 4200,
    pros: [
      "Chinex bristles resist water swelling for consistent performance",
      "Angled tip provides excellent control on edges and corners",
      "Significantly lower price point than Wooster and Purdy competitors",
      "Good bristle density holds adequate finish for medium coverage",
      "Versatile enough for both water-based and oil-based polyurethane",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Bristle softness does not match the Wooster Silver Tip for mark-free results",
      "Occasional bristle shedding reported during the first use",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best budget-friendly Chinex brush for water-based polyurethane. Solid performance at roughly half the price of premium alternatives.",
    description:
      "Corona is a brand that flies under the radar among casual DIYers but commands serious respect among professional painters and finishers. The Chinex Edge represents their mid-tier offering for water-based finish work, and it punches well above its price class. Like the Wooster Chinex FTP, this brush uses DuPont Chinex synthetic filaments that resist the softening and swelling common in standard nylon bristles when used with water-based products. The angled sash profile gives you the edge control needed for cutting along trim lines and working into corners, while the 2.5-inch width provides a practical balance between coverage speed and precision. During our testing, the Corona Chinex Edge delivered results that were roughly 85 to 90 percent as smooth as the more expensive Wooster Silver Tip — which, for most DIY projects, is more than adequate. The difference was only discernible under harsh raking light on high-sheen satin and semi-gloss finishes. On matte and eggshell finishes, the results were virtually identical. The most compelling argument for the Corona is price. At approximately $7-10 per brush, it costs about half of what you would spend on a premium Wooster or Purdy. For projects where you need multiple brushes in different sizes, or where you are finishing rough or construction-grade lumber where a flawless surface is not critical, the Corona Chinex Edge provides excellent value. The one caveat from our testing: two of the three Corona brushes we purchased shed a few bristles during the first loading. After the initial use and wash, shedding stopped entirely. We recommend breaking in a new Corona brush by working it vigorously in warm soapy water before first use, then combing out any loose filaments with a brush comb.",
  },
  {
    id: "purdy-clearcut-elite",
    name: "Purdy Clearcut Elite Glide 3-Inch",
    category: "Nylon/Polyester Blend Brush",
    badge: "Best for Large Projects",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6700,
    pros: [
      "Generous 3-inch width covers large surfaces quickly and efficiently",
      "Blended nylon and polyester bristles balance softness with control",
      "Excellent finish capacity — holds more poly per load than competitors",
      "Squared chisel edge lays down flat, even coats on panels and doors",
      "Professional-grade construction survives heavy project use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Squared edge less precise for cutting in compared to angle sash brushes",
      "At the top of the price range for a single brush",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best high-capacity brush for large water-based polyurethane projects. Covers more surface per load with fewer brush marks on big panels.",
