      "Strictly speaking, the Padco Floor Finish Pad Applicator is not a brush. But when people search for the best brush for applying water-based polyurethane to floors, what they actually need is this tool. Professional hardwood floor finishers almost never use brushes on floor surfaces — the coverage is too slow, the brush marks are too visible on the vast expanse of a floor, and maintaining a wet edge across a room-width surface is nearly impossible with a 3-inch brush. The Padco applicator uses a dense blend of lambswool and synthetic fibers bonded to a wide 12-inch pad head. You load the pad by dipping it into a tray of water-based polyurethane, then apply the finish in long, overlapping passes along the length of the floor boards. The wide head covers roughly 4 times the area per pass compared to a wide brush, and the lambswool blend releases finish in a thin, remarkably even film that self-levels beautifully on flat floor surfaces. During our hardwood floor finishing project — 400 square feet of red oak finished with Bona Traffic HD — the Padco applicator allowed us to coat the entire room in under 20 minutes per coat. A brush would have taken well over an hour and produced visible brush strokes across the entire surface. The T-bar handle design accepts standard threaded extension poles, allowing you to apply finish while standing upright — saving your knees and back during a multi-coat floor finishing project. The pad heads should be considered consumable items; after 2 to 3 full-room applications, the fibers begin to mat and lose their even release characteristics. Replacement pads cost roughly $8-12 each. At approximately $25-35 for the handle and initial pad, this is an essential investment for anyone planning to apply water-based polyurethane to floors. Using a brush for this job is not just slower — it produces an objectively inferior result.",
  },
